    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Diving deeper into technical solutions, considering both packet loss and potential interference, primarily on the 2.4GHz band, here's a structured multi-step approach. First, Reassess the current WiFi channel usage to evaluate and potentially lower the interference suffered due to neighbor networks’ broadcasts. Use a WiFi analyzer to distinguish which channels are less congested. Choose a channel that falls outside of the dedicated non-overlapping channels (1, 6, and 11 in the 2.4GHz band in the U.S.).\n\nFor packet loss, which might be linked to interference and high network load, employ Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) alongside your existing WAN technology if possible to streamline data packets’ routing decisions. Evaluate your QoS settings; segregating traffic types prioritizing real-time applications could alleviate congestion.\n\nNext, conduct a site survey to detect the Interference Signal to Noise Ratio (SNR). Utilize spectrum analysis tools to gauge the presence of extraneous signals causing attenuation. Upgrading the hardware to routers and access points with superior range and Atheros or Qualcomm chips is recommended since they are typically more adept at handling 2.4GHz band interference.\n\nIn back rooms known for interference hotspots, consider deploying additional access points",
